node_modules/
dist/
build/
.next/
out/
coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Config files. Match every variant the toolchains load (.env.local,
# .env.production, .env.development.local, ...) rather than enumerating base
# names — enumerating is how .env.local ends up committed. The example files
# are the deliberate exception.
.env*
!.env.example

# Expo / React Native
.expo/
.expo-shared/
*.jks
*.p8
*.p12
*.key
*.mobileprovision
ios/
android/

# Map data — too big for git, host it instead
*.pmtiles
*.mbtiles

.DS_Store
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.idea/
.vscode/

# MapLibre ships its tile-parsing worker as a separate module that a bundler
# cannot serve for it. apps/web/scripts/copy-maplibre-worker.mjs copies it out of
# node_modules on every dev and build, so the copy is generated, never edited,
# and can never drift from the installed version.
apps/web/public/maplibre/

# Impeccable. `design.json` is the design-system sidecar and is committed beside
# DESIGN.md; `config.local.json` is per-machine state (hook consent, local
# overrides) and is not.
.impeccable/config.local.json

# The skill itself is not tracked anywhere. It is installed per-machine and
# reinstalled at whatever version its installer is on, so committing it pins a
# version nobody chose and — since 0.1.5 — a 12 MB `darwin-arm64` binary that is
# useless to anyone not on an Apple Silicon Mac. `DESIGN.md` and `design.json` are
# the outputs worth keeping, and they are committed; the tool that produced them
# is not. The hook is excluded for a second reason: it runs the detector after
# every edit, which changes a contributor's edit loop rather than this
# repository's contents.
#
# Named rather than ignored by directory, so an unrelated skill, agent or hook
# added later is not silently swallowed. The installer writes one directory per
# provider it knows — `.claude`, `.codex`, `.cursor`, `.gemini`, `.github`,
# `.agents` — so add the path here when you install into a new one.
.claude/skills/impeccable/
.claude/agents/impeccable-*.md
.codex/skills/impeccable/
.codex/agents/impeccable-*.toml
.github/skills/impeccable/
.github/agents/impeccable-*.agent.md
.github/hooks/impeccable.json

# `.agents/skills/` is written by two installers, so nothing in it is ours unless
# this file says so. The rule is inverted here: ignore every entry, then name the
# ones the repository owns.
#
# `.agents/skills/.openspec-target` reads `codex`, and that is what makes this the
# right home for a Codex-facing skill rather than `.codex/` — it is the directory
# OpenSpec writes for its Codex, Zed and shared-agents targets, and the one those
# runtimes read. `pinpoint-explore` lives here for that reason and is committed.
#
# Everything else here is a generated second copy. Impeccable's installer mirrors
# its skill from `.claude/`, with its script paths rewritten to a directory that
# does not exist. `openspec update` leaves `openspec-*` skills and
# `source-command-opsx-*` — its own commands restated as skills, for a runtime with
# no slash commands. Those are the ones to be wary of: they are regenerated at
# whatever OpenSpec is on PATH, which is not necessarily the 1.13.0 this repository
# pins, and a 1.7.0 copy still carries the "think deeply" / "brainstorm multiple
# approaches" / "diagrams liberally" wording that AGENTS.md § Talking to the user
# overrides and that #117 removed from `.claude/`.
#
# The whole of `.agents/` is ignored, not just `skills/` — the installers write
# plugins, lock files and caches there too, and none of it is ours. Re-inclusion
# has to step down one level at a time: git will not look inside an excluded
# directory, so each ancestor of a kept path must be un-excluded before the next
# `*` can be applied.
.agents/*
!.agents/skills/
.agents/skills/*
!.agents/skills/pinpoint-explore/

# Generated design mocks. The builder is committed; its ~290 KB output, which
# embeds the bundled font, is not — it regenerates in under a second.
openspec/changes/**/mock/*.html

# Things an agent noticed while working on a change, held until the change is
# archived and then either taken up or dropped. See AGENTS.md.
openspec/changes/**/findings.md
